Why Are Optical Interconnects Important for AI Infrastructure?

AI workloads require substantial data movement between compute processors, memory systems and network switches. As cluster size and bandwidth requirements increase, optical technologies can help transmit data over distances and at capacities that are challenging for electrical links.

Optical interconnects convert electrical data into optical signals and back again, enabling high-speed communication within and between computing systems. Their commercial value depends on bandwidth, energy efficiency, latency, reliability, thermal characteristics and total system cost.

Silicon Photonics Is Reshaping Interconnect Design

Silicon photonic links are an important market category because photonic components can be integrated with semiconductor manufacturing processes and positioned close to compute or switching silicon.

The wider industry is investigating pluggable optics, on-board optics, near-package optics and co-packaged optics. Each architecture involves different trade-offs in serviceability, packaging complexity, thermal management and electrical reach.

Data-Center Interconnects Lead Application Demand

Data-center interconnects are a key application as hyperscale operators expand AI training and inference infrastructure. High-performance computing and edge AI accelerators also require reliable communication links, although deployment requirements vary by architecture and workload.

Optical connectivity can reduce some limitations associated with long, high-speed copper links. However, power consumption in optical engines, laser integration, packaging yield, manufacturing cost and system-level interoperability remain important engineering considerations.

Co-Packaged Optics and Higher Bandwidth Density

Co-packaged optics brings optical engines closer to switching or computing silicon to reduce the length of high-speed electrical connections. The approach can improve bandwidth density and power efficiency in suitable systems, but it also introduces challenges in thermal design, optical alignment, maintenance and manufacturing.

Continued progress in silicon photonics, lasers, modulators, optical packaging and high-speed SerDes is influencing the development of next-generation optical interconnect platforms.

